(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-2-heart-great-vessels-quiz"]={"title":"Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels — Anatomy Exam 2","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"How many chambers does the heart consist of, and what are the two circuits it pumps blood into?","choices":["Four chambers; pumps into only the pulmonary circuit, with systemic circulation handled separately","Two chambers; pumps into only the systemic circuit via a single ventricular pump","Three chambers; pumps into the coronary and pulmonary circuits simultaneously","Four chambers (two atria, two ventricles); pumps into the pulmonary circuit and systemic circuit"],"answer":3,"exp":"The heart consists of four chambers (two atria and two ventricles) and pumps blood into two circuits: the pulmonary circuit and the systemic circuit. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What two layers does the pericardial membrane form, and what reinforces the parietal pericardium?","choices":["Visceral pericardium (epicardium) and parietal pericardium; the parietal pericardium is reinforced by the fibrous pericardium","Visceral and parietal pericardium, both reinforced by the myocardium's outer muscular fibers","Endocardium and myocardium, reinforced by a thin layer of areolar connective tissue","Only one layer: the epicardium, reinforced directly by the fibrous pericardium beneath it"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pericardial membrane forms two layers: the visceral pericardium (also called the epicardium) and the parietal pericardium; the parietal pericardium is reinforced by a layer called the fibrous pericardium. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the normal amount of pericardial fluid, and where is it contained?","choices":["No fluid is normally present within the pericardial sac at rest","500-1000 mL, contained within the heart chambers themselves","5 mL, contained within the myocardium's muscular layer","15-35 mL, contained between the visceral and parietal pericardial layers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Pericardial fluid, a type of serous fluid, is normally present in an amount of 15-35 mL, contained between the visceral and parietal pericardial layers. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What are the functions of the pericardium?","choices":["Only produces hormones, such as atrial natriuretic peptide, in response to stretch","Only conducts electrical impulses between the atria and ventricles","Only pumps blood through the coronary vessels during diastole, independent of ventricular contraction","Protects and anchors the heart, prevents overfilling with blood, and allows friction-free heart movement"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pericardium protects and anchors the heart, prevents overfilling of the heart with blood, and allows the heart to work in a relatively friction-free environment. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What are the three layers of the heart wall, from external to internal?","choices":["Epicardium, myocardium, endocardium","Endocardium, myocardium, epicardium","Pericardium, myocardium, endocardium","Only two layers: myocardium and endocardium"],"answer":0,"exp":"The heart wall consists of three layers, from external to internal: the epicardium (external surface), the myocardium (consisting of cardiomyocytes), and the endocardium (internal surface). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What are intercalated discs, and what two types of cell junctions do they contain?","choices":["A type of heart valve, unrelated to cell junctions, located between the atria and ventricles","Specialized cell-to-cell junctions between cardiac cells, containing desmosomes (binding actin) and gap junctions (allowing ion movement between cells)","Found only in skeletal muscle, not cardiac muscle, connecting adjacent myofibrils end to end","A single type of junction: tight junctions only, sealing adjacent cardiac cells completely and preventing any ion exchange between them"],"answer":1,"exp":"Intercalated discs are specialized cell-to-cell junctions in cardiac cells, containing desmosomes (primarily desmoglein 2, binding actin of adjacent cells) and gap junctions (allowing ions to move directly from one cell to another, letting all sarcomeres contract as one unit). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is the sarcolemma, and what wraps each muscle layer of the heart?","choices":["Sarcolemma is a type of heart valve; muscle layers are wrapped in epithelial tissue lining the endocardium","Sarcolemma is unrelated to cardiac muscle cells, functioning instead as part of the fibrous skeleton","Sarcolemma is the elastic sheath wrapping each cardiac cell; each muscle layer is wrapped in a fibrous sheet of dense, regular connective tissue (collagen)","Sarcolemma is the heart's electrical conduction pathway, carrying impulses between the SA and AV nodes"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sarcolemma is the elastic sheath wrapping each cardiac cell, and each muscle layer is wrapped in a fibrous sheet made of dense, regular connective tissue (collagen); the fibrous sheets separate superficial from deep layer muscles. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What are the functions of the fibrous skeleton of the heart?","choices":["Stabilizes cardiac cells and heart valves, supports vessels/nerves in the myocardium, distributes contraction forces, prevents overexpansion, provides elastic recoil, and isolates atrial from ventricular cells","Only produces blood cells, supplying the myocardium with a continuous source of new erythrocytes and leukocytes, much like bone marrow does","Only forms the pericardial sac, providing a protective, friction-free outer covering for the heart without any internal structural support","Only conducts electrical impulses, functioning as the sole pathway linking the SA node to the ventricles, without any connective role elsewhere"],"answer":0,"exp":"The fibrous skeleton stabilizes the position of cardiac cells and heart valves, provides support for blood vessels/nerves in the myocardium, helps distribute the forces of contraction, prevents overexpansion of the heart, provides elasticity for recoil after contraction, and isolates atrial cells from ventricular cells. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Where is the heart located, and what vertebral levels does it span in most adults?","choices":["Entirely to the right of midline, spanning C3-C6","Exactly at midline, in the abdominal cavity, spanning L2-L4","In the pelvic cavity, spanning S1 to S5 in most adults","Slightly left of midline, in the mediastinum, spanning ~T4-T8/9"],"answer":3,"exp":"The heart lies slightly to the left of midline, located in the mediastinum, spanning approximately T4 to T8/9 in most adults. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the base of the heart, and what is the apex?","choices":["Base is the inferior portion; apex is the superior portion","Base is the superior portion; apex is the inferior portion","Both terms describe the same location","Base and apex both refer to the left ventricle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The base of the heart is the superior portion, and the apex is the inferior portion. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which chamber is the most anterior structure of the heart itself?","choices":["The left ventricle","The left atrium","The right ventricle","The right atrium"],"answer":2,"exp":"The right ventricle is the most anterior structure of the heart itself. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What forms the right border of the heart, and what forms the posterior surface?","choices":["Right border: right ventricle only; Posterior surface: right atrium","Right border: left atrium; Posterior surface: right ventricle","Right border: right atrium only; Posterior surface: left atrium","Both the right border and posterior surface are formed by the same chamber"],"answer":2,"exp":"The right border of the heart is formed by only the right atrium, while the posterior surface is formed by the left atrium. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What structures are positioned superior to the coronary sulcus, and what is true of both atria?","choices":["The left and right ventricles; both have thick, muscular walls compared to the atria above them","The left and right atria; both have thin walls and expandable extensions called auricles (atrial appendages)","The aorta and pulmonary trunk, both lacking any expandable auricular appendages","Only the right atrium; it has no auricle, unlike the muscular left atrium beside it"],"answer":1,"exp":"The left and right atria are positioned superior to the coronary sulcus; both have thin walls and consist of expandable extensions called auricles or atrial appendages. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What three vessels does the right atrium receive deoxygenated blood from?","choices":["Pulmonary veins, pulmonary trunk, and aorta","Pulmonary trunk and pulmonary veins only","Only the inferior vena cava, entering near the diaphragm","Superior vena cava, inferior vena cava, and coronary sinus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The right atrium receives deoxygenated blood via the superior vena cava, inferior vena cava, and coronary sinus. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is the fossa ovalis, and what fetal structure is it a remnant of?","choices":["A valve structure, not a remnant of any fetal structure","A fetal remnant of the foramen ovale","A structure found only in the left atrium","A fetal remnant of the ductus arteriosus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The fossa ovalis, found in the right atrium, is a fetal remnant of the foramen ovale. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What are pectinate muscles, and what does the crista terminalis do?","choices":["Pectinate muscles are \"comb-like\" structures pushing blood unidirectionally; the crista terminalis serves as an origin of the pectinate muscles","Pectinate muscles conduct electrical impulses directly to the AV node; crista terminalis is a valve","Pectinate muscles are found in the right ventricle only; crista terminalis regulates pressure","Pectinate muscles are smooth, without any comb-like structure, and lack a defined origin point"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pectinate muscles are \"comb-like\" structures in the right atrium, structured to push blood unidirectionally; the crista terminalis serves as an origin of the pectinate muscles. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What hormone can be released if the right atrial appendage (auricle) becomes overfilled?","choices":["Aldosterone, from the adrenal cortex","Cortisol, from the adrenal cortex","Renin, from the juxtaglomerular cells","Brain (atrial) natriuretic peptide"],"answer":3,"exp":"If the right atrial appendage (auricle), which holds extra blood volume, becomes overfilled, it can stimulate the release of brain (atrial) natriuretic peptide. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Through which valve does blood pass from the right atrium into the right ventricle, and how many papillary muscles is it connected to?","choices":["Aortic valve, a semilunar valve connected to no papillary muscles or chordae tendineae whatsoever","Bicuspid (mitral) valve, connected via chordae tendineae to two papillary muscles matching its two cusps","Tricuspid valve (right AV valve), connected via chordae tendineae to three papillary muscles (matching its three cusps)","Pulmonary valve, a semilunar valve connected to four separate papillary muscles within the right ventricle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Blood enters the right ventricle by passing through the tricuspid valve (right atrioventricular valve); since there are three cusps, the chordae tendineae connect to three papillary muscles. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is the moderator band, and where is it found?","choices":["A muscular band extending from the interventricular septum to the ventricular wall, containing Purkinje fibers, found only in the right ventricle","A band found only in the atria, conducting impulses directly between the SA node and AV node across the interatrial septum","A structure found equally in both ventricles, containing bundle branch fibers that conduct impulses toward the apex","A type of heart valve regulating flow between the right atrium and right ventricle, preventing any backflow of blood"],"answer":0,"exp":"The moderator band is a muscular band that extends from the interventricular septum to the ventricular wall, containing conduction tissue called Purkinje fibers; it is found only in the right ventricle, mainly serving as a conductor. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What are trabeculae carneae (\"bands of meat\"), and what is their function?","choices":["Blood vessels supplying the right ventricle's myocardium directly from the coronary circulation","A type of heart valve located between the right atrium and right ventricle","Internal surface structures of the right ventricle, structured to contract the ventricle from all directions","Structures found only in the atria, forming a comb-like ridge along the crista terminalis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Trabeculae carneae (\"bands of meat\") are internal surface structures of the right ventricle, structured to contract the ventricle from all directions. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What does the left atrium receive, and through what does blood pass to reach the left ventricle?","choices":["Receives oxygenated blood from the lungs via the pulmonary veins; blood passes through the bicuspid (mitral) valve","Receives oxygenated blood via the coronary sinus; blood passes through the pulmonary valve","Receives deoxygenated blood via the pulmonary trunk; blood passes through the tricuspid valve","Receives blood from the venae cavae directly; blood passes through the aortic valve"],"answer":0,"exp":"The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the lungs via the right and left pulmonary veins; blood passes through the bicuspid valve (left atrioventricular valve, also called the mitral valve) to reach the left ventricle. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Why does the left ventricle have the thickest myocardial tissue, described as the \"workhorse of the heart\"?","choices":["It pumps blood only a short distance through the pulmonary circuit, requiring far less force than the right ventricle actually needs","Both ventricles have identical wall thickness, since they generate equal pressure during systole regardless of circuit resistance","It needs strong contractions to pump blood throughout the entire systemic vascular circuit, unlike the right ventricle which only pumps through the pulmonary circuit","It has the thinnest walls of any chamber, since the low-resistance pulmonary circuit demands very little contractile force to move blood"],"answer":2,"exp":"The left ventricle has the thickest myocardial tissue because it needs strong contractions to pump blood throughout the entire systemic vascular circuit, while the right ventricle has a thin wall since it only pumps blood through the (shorter, lower-pressure) pulmonary circuit. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What are the two papillary muscles of the left ventricle's AV valve?","choices":["Anterolateral papillary muscle and posteromedial papillary muscle","Anterior and posterior papillary muscles (three total, matching three cusps)","Medial and lateral papillary muscles, four total","Only one papillary muscle, matching the bicuspid valve's single cusp"],"answer":0,"exp":"The left ventricle's AV valve (bicuspid/mitral) has chordae tendineae connecting to two cusps and two papillary muscles: the anterolateral papillary muscle and the posteromedial papillary muscle. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Through which valve does blood leave the left ventricle, and where does it go next?","choices":["Pulmonary valve, entering the pulmonary trunk toward the lungs","Aortic valve, entering the ascending aorta, then the aortic arch, then to all body parts (systemic)","Mitral valve, entering the left atrium in a retrograde direction","Tricuspid valve, entering the right atrium from the left side"],"answer":1,"exp":"Blood leaves the left ventricle by passing through the aortic valve, entering the ascending aorta, then traveling to the aortic arch and then to all body parts (the systemic circuit). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What four parts make up each AV valve?","choices":["Endocardium, myocardium, epicardium, and pericardium","Ring of connective tissue, cusps, chordae tendineae, and papillary muscles","Ring of connective tissue and cusps only, without any tendinous attachments","Only cusps and chordae tendineae, lacking any supporting papillary muscles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Each AV valve consists of four parts: a ring of connective tissue connecting to the heart tissue, cusps, chordae tendineae, and papillary muscles. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What causes the AV valves to close, and what happens as a result?","choices":["Blood forces the AV valves closed, resulting in blood going through the semilunar valves (pulmonary and aortic) instead, preventing backflow","The AV valves never close during the cardiac cycle, remaining open throughout systole and diastole","Papillary muscle relaxation forces the AV valves closed during ventricular systole, preventing any backflow of blood","The semilunar valves force the AV valves closed by generating backward pressure during ventricular diastole"],"answer":0,"exp":"Blood forces the AV valves closed, resulting in blood going through the semilunar valves (pulmonary and aortic) instead, preventing backflow of blood; papillary muscles contract to prevent valvular prolapse and maintain tension. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"During ventricular diastole, are the AV valves open or closed, and are the semilunar valves open or closed?","choices":["AV valves are closed; semilunar valves are open","Both sets of valves are open simultaneously","AV valves are open; semilunar valves are closed","Both sets of valves are closed simultaneously"],"answer":2,"exp":"During ventricular diastole (when the ventricles are relaxed), the AV valves are open and the semilunar valves are closed, with the chordae tendineae loose and papillary muscles relaxed. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"During ventricular systole, are the AV valves open or closed, and are the semilunar valves open or closed?","choices":["AV valves are open; semilunar valves are closed","Both sets of valves remain open throughout systole","Neither valve set changes state during systole","AV valves are closed; semilunar valves are open"],"answer":3,"exp":"During ventricular systole (when the ventricles are contracting), the AV valves are closed and the semilunar valves are open, with the chordae tendineae tense and papillary muscles contracted. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the coronary plexus, and what two autonomic components does it consist of?","choices":["The heart's autonomic innervation network, consisting of sympathetic and parasympathetic components","A structure found only within the SA node, regulating its intrinsic firing rate","A structure consisting only of sympathetic fibers, entirely lacking parasympathetic input","A network of coronary blood vessels only, unrelated to nerves or autonomic control"],"answer":0,"exp":"The heart is innervated by the autonomic nervous system, referred to as the coronary plexus, consisting of both sympathetic and parasympathetic components; this innervation can increase or decrease (but does not initiate) the heartbeat. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which nerve is the exception that only innervates the SA and AV nodes (not cardiomyocytes generally), and what is the right versus left tendency?","choices":["Sympathetic cardiac nerves; both sides innervate only the SA node exclusively","Phrenic nerve; left phrenic innervates the SA node, right phrenic innervates the AV node","Phrenic nerve; innervates only the AV node, sparing the SA node entirely","Vagus nerve (parasympathetic); right vagus tends to innervate the SA node, left vagus tends to innervate the AV node"],"answer":3,"exp":"The vagal nerve (parasympathetic) is the exception, only innervating the SA and AV nodes; the right vagus nerve tends to innervate the SA node, while the left vagus nerve tends to innervate the AV node (with significant anatomical variation). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 66"}]};
